\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-2
Across six METABENCH benchmarks, 17.9--31.3\% of items exhibit temporal DIF across 2023--2024 model cohorts (${\approx}$27--29\% for MMLU; conservative bound: 13.5\%), while aggregate rankings remain preserved ($\rho \approx 0.997$). Global exposure and differential functioning are complementary diagnostic dimensions (Jaccard $= 0.206$), confirmed by opposite base-vs-instruct DIF patterns ($\chi^2 = 562.7$). DIF-corrected scoring shifts accuracy by ${+}2.12$\,pp on average, with rank displacements exceeding ${\pm}500$. Mechanism-conditional DIF audit should complement contamination screening as standard practice~\citep{habba2026growingpains}.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix~\citep{kipnis2024metabench} (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items~\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}), with temporal cohorts defined by release date (2023 vs.\ 2024). Cross-benchmark validation across all six METABENCH benchmarks appears in \Cref{sec:gsm8k}.

\subsection{Validation: DIF Detection Power}
\label{sec:validation}

\looseness=-1
A Monte Carlo power analysis (1,000 replications) using empirical 2PL parameters from PSN-IRT~\citep{zhou2026psnirt} ($\bar{a} = 1.2$, $\bar{b} = -0.84$) confirms that at $N \geq 80$ per cohort, ETS~C achieves TPR of 0.68--0.80 and AUC of 0.93--0.94 for $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.4$ (\Cref{app:method_details}). Our temporal cohorts ($N_{\mathrm{ref}} = 1{,}080$, $N_{\mathrm{focal}} = 807$) far exceed this threshold; power varies with item discrimination (TPR 0.40--0.95 across $a = 0.5$--$2.0$; \Cref{app:power_sensitivity}).

\subsection{Temporal DIF: 31\% of Items Exhibit Differential Functioning}
\label{sec:temporal}

\looseness=-1
We compare 2023 ($N = 1{,}080$) against 2024 ($N = 807$) models; 31.3\% of items receive ETS~C classification ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$; bootstrap 95\% CI: $[29.4\%, 38.1\%]$; family-level cluster bootstrap: $[26.7\%, 53.2\%]$, upper bound driven by mega-family over-representation; \Cref{app:uncertainty}). Two DEFF estimators bound the non-independence effect in parallel: simple-mean DEFF (29.2\%) and cluster bootstrap (26.7\%) converge on ${\approx}$27--29\%, while the conservative weighted-mean DEFF yields 13.5\%---still ${\approx}22\times$ the random baseline (\Cref{app:deff,app:deff_simulation}). We recommend simple-mean DEFF as the primary estimate: it converges with the cluster bootstrap and DEFF simulation, while weighted-mean DEFF over-corrects by down-weighting large families with systematically lower ICC. Under 100 random permutations within the 2023 cohort (540 vs.\ 540), the 99th percentile of item-level $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}|$ is 1.45---just below the ETS~C threshold of 1.5---confirming that the threshold corresponds to a per-item false-positive rate below 1\% in the LLM setting. No single family drives the signal (LOFO $|\Delta\mathrm{C\%}| \leq 2.93$\,pp); graduated de-duplication confirms C\% scales with power, not redundancy (\Cref{app:dedup_k}).

\looseness=-1
Three controls confirm genuine temporal change (\Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}): random splits yield mean C\% of 0.62\% (2023; 100 permutations) and 1.3\% (2024; 100 permutations), both ${\geq}24\times$ below temporal C\%; ability-quintile comparison yields 2.9\%; and 1:1 nearest-neighbor matching on total score ($N{=}638$/group, Cohen's $d = -0.17$) yields C\% $= 28.9\% \pm 0.1\%$, confirming at most ${\sim}2.4$~pp is attributable to ability mismatch ($p < 0.01$; \Cref{app:method_details}).

\looseness=-1
Across all 57 subjects, C\% ranges from 20.7\% to 48.4\% (mean $\approx 30\%$; \Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}). DIF rates are domain-invariant (Kruskal-Wallis $p = 0.78$), but DIF \emph{direction} exhibits domain dependence: STEM items favor 2024 models (55.4\%), Humanities favor 2023 (64.4\%). Domain-level accuracy gains explain ${<}0.1\%$ of item-level DIF variance (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$); a Simpson's paradox confirms DIF is associated with item properties rather than aggregate gains (OR reverses from 1.55 to 0.73 after controlling for difficulty; \Cref{app:structural}). A difficulty--direction interaction emerges ($\rho = -0.597$): 2024 models gain on hard items while eroding on easy ones. The conclusion is robust across thresholds ($18.3\%$ at $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 2.0$, $10.2\%$ at $\geq 2.5$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{DIF landscape across cohort definitions. ETS~C denotes the percentage of items with $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$. C\% (web-clean) is the DIF-C flagging rate among items classified as web-clean by \citet{li2024contamination}. Enrichment is the ratio of observed to expected overlap between DIF flags and web-overlap labels. Dashes (--) indicate comparisons where Li et al.\ labels are not applicable.}
\label{tab:temporal_landscape}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Cohort Definition & $N_{\mathrm{ref}}$ / $N_{\mathrm{focal}}$ & C\% & C\% (web-clean) & Enrich. \\
\midrule
Temporal (2023 vs.\ 2024) & 1,080 / 807 & 31.3 & 0.318 & 0.964 \\
Ability quintile (Q1--2 vs.\ Q4--5) & ${\sim}$2,600 / ${\sim}$2,600 & 2.9 & 0.027 & 1.19 \\
Within-family (Llama-2 vs.\ 3) & 161 / 308 & 61.3 & -- & -- \\
Random split 2023 (sanity) & 540 / 540 & 0.62 & -- & -- \\
Random split 2024 (sanity) & ${\sim}$400 / ${\sim}$400 & 1.3 & -- & -- \\
Within-subject (avg, 5 subj.) & \textit{varies} & 38.1 & -- & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{Ground Truth Mismatch}
\label{sec:groundtruth}

\looseness=-1
Direct comparison of DIF flags against the web-overlap labels of \citet{li2024contamination} on 9,229 annotated items confirms that the two methods capture orthogonal signals---an empirical verification of the theoretically expected but previously untested distinction between global exposure and differential functioning (\Cref{fig:global_vs_differential}). The enrichment ratio is 0.964 (bootstrap 95\% CI: $[0.94, 1.02]$; \Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}): DIF-C rates are 30.7\% among web-contaminated items and 31.8\% among clean items. Association tests confirm near-independence ($\phi = 0.012$, OR $= 1.05$ [0.96, 1.15], Jaccard $= 0.206$, within permutation null $[0.203, 0.222]$; $p = 0.88$). This near-independence holds across contamination subtypes. The null result does not indicate detection failure: semi-synthetic injection produces a clear dose-response ($\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.508$ at $p = 0.5$; \Cref{fig:dose_response}). The disconnect arises because web-overlap labels measure \emph{global exposure} (whether an item exists in training corpora), while DIF measures \emph{differential functioning} (whether an item's relationship to ability changes across cohorts). The two constructs---global exposure and differential functioning---are empirically orthogonal.
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_2_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Semi-synthetic dose-response for DIF detection on MMLU. $\Delta\mathrm{TPR}$ increases monotonically with exploitation rate $p$, confirming that MH-DIF is sensitive to differential item behavior when present. Baseline (no injection) produces $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} \approx 0$.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Structural Analysis}
\label{sec:structural}

\looseness=-1
Two structural controls and a sensitivity check fail to reduce temporal C\% below 31\% (\Cref{tab:structural_comparison}): matched-ability quintiles, within-subject DIF (C\% $= 38.1\%$; cross-subject C\% is more conservative due to lower local dependence), and IRT $\theta$-conditioning ($r(\theta, \mathrm{total\ score}) = 0.9986$). Logistic regression detects an additional 17.7\% with non-uniform DIF ($|\beta_{\mathrm{interaction}}| \geq 0.5$), reaching a union of 47\% (\Cref{app:structural}). Ability-matched C\% converges to ${\sim}$28--29\% for both temporal and within-family comparisons despite vastly different unmatched rates (\Cref{app:structural}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Structural controls and sensitivity checks for temporal DIF. None reduces C\% below 31\%; $\theta$-conditioning serves as a sensitivity check confirming total-score adequacy.}
\label{tab:structural_comparison}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Approach & Mechanism & Temp.\ C\% & TPR ($p{=}0.5$) & Outcome \\
\midrule
Baseline (ext.) & Cross-bench.\ total score & $0.314_{\pm.009}$ & $0.834_{\pm.027}$ & Reference \\
Matched-ability & Within-quintile temp.\ DIF & ${\sim}$0.31 & -- & Enrich.\ 0.94--1.04 \\
$\theta$-cond.\ (sens.) & $\theta$ replaces total score & 0.312 & 0.876 & $r(\theta,\mathrm{total}) {=} 0.9986$ \\
Within-subject & Per-subject DIF & -- & -- & C\% $= 38.1\%$ ($>$25\%) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{Detection Power under Controlled Injection}
\label{sec:semisynthetic}

\looseness=-1
Cross-subject matching achieves $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0.000$ across all five MMLU subjects (4/5 with detection-power upper bounds of $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} > 0.3$ at $p = 0.5$; stylized uniform injection; \Cref{sec:injection}), while within-subject matching inflates FPR by ${+}0.186$ on average. The $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} = 0$ guarantee holds for single-subject contamination ($k \leq 5$, $\Delta\mathrm{FPR} < 0.007$). Under broader contamination spanning $k = 10$ subjects (18\% of domains), $\Delta\mathrm{FPR}$ rises to $0.047 \pm 0.022$; at $k = 20$, to $0.069 \pm 0.018$ (\Cref{tab:multi_subject_injection,app:fig_matching}). Even worst-case multi-subject inflation ($\sim$7~pp) cannot account for the observed 31\% C\% rate, which exceeds the 0.62\% random baseline by ${>}50\times$. Under DEFF adjustment, worst-case $\Delta\mathrm{FPR}$ at $k{=}20$ (0.069) represents up to 50\% of the 13.5\% lower bound, underscoring $k \leq 5$ in practice. Difficulty-dependent injection (non-uniform flipping weighted by item difficulty) yields $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} \approx 0.41$ at $p = 0.5$ (vs.\ $0.51$ uniform), with distinguishable $\rho$ signatures (capability-like: $+0.77$ vs.\ contamination-like: $-0.52$; zero seed overlap), though mapping to real-data mechanisms requires further work.

\subsection{Downstream Impact}
\label{sec:downstream}

\looseness=-1
Stable-item ranks are preserved across all six benchmarks ($\rho = 0.997$, $\tau = 0.958$; flip rate $\leq 2.8\%$; \Cref{tab:cross_benchmark}). DIF-flagged items concentrate discriminative signal ($z = -8$ to $-49$ vs.\ random removal; \Cref{app:fig_downstream}). Among the 200 most displaced models (top 3.8\%), 33.6\% of pairwise rankings reverse, concentrating among closely-ranked pairs (35.2\% at $\Delta\mathrm{rank} < 50$ vs.\ 0.0\% above 1000; \Cref{app:rank_reversal}). Removing C-flagged items disproportionately disadvantages 2024 models (cohort gap $= 96.2$ positions, $40\times$ random; $p < 0.001$). DIF-corrected scoring (stable items only) shifts accuracy by ${+}2.12$\,pp on average, with a cohort-differential of $0.29$\,pp ($d = 0.31$; individual rank displacements reach ${\pm}800$; \Cref{app:score_inflation}).

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation}
\label{sec:gsm8k}

\looseness=-1
Temporal DIF extends across all six METABENCH benchmarks (\Cref{tab:cross_benchmark}): C\% ranges from 17.9\% (HellaSwag~\citep{zellers2019hellaswag}) to 31.3\% (MMLU), with knowledge/reasoning benchmarks consistently higher than procedural ones (C\% $= 26.9$--$31.3\%$ vs.\ $17.9$--$18.9\%$). These differences may partly reflect item difficulty distributions; we treat them as descriptive.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Temporal DIF across six METABENCH benchmarks. C\% = ETS~C items ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024 cohorts; $\rho$/$\tau$ = Spearman/Kendall correlation between full and stable-item rankings; Flip = pairwise rank-flip rate; Ratio = C\%/Rand.\ C\%, with baseline varying by benchmark. All six show substantial DIF (45--95$\times$ above random baseline), with knowledge/reasoning benchmarks exhibiting higher C\% than procedural ones.}
\label{tab:cross_benchmark}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrrl}
\toprule
Benchmark & Items & C\% & $\rho$ & $\tau$ & Flip\% & Rand.\ C\% & Ratio \\
\midrule
MMLU & 12{,}508 & 31.3 & 0.9965 & 0.9600 & 2.00 & 0.62 & 50$\times$ \\
WinoGrande & 1{,}267 & 30.3 & 0.9943 & 0.9432 & 2.84 & 0.32 & 95$\times$ \\
TruthfulQA & 817 & 29.7 & 0.9971 & 0.9586 & 2.07 & 0.37 & 80$\times$ \\
ARC & 1{,}172 & 26.9 & 0.9962 & 0.9538 & 2.31 & 0.60 & 45$\times$ \\
GSM8K & 1{,}319 & 18.9 & 0.9993 & 0.9843 & 0.78 & 0.30 & 63$\times$ \\
HellaSwag & 10{,}042 & 17.9 & 0.9986 & 0.9722 & 1.39 & 0.28 & 64$\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Semi-synthetic injection on GSM8K is consistent with detection power ($\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.514$; \Cref{app:gsm8k_details}). As an exploratory cross-benchmark signal, GSM1K contamination gaps correlate with DIF-C item accuracy ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$, $N = 33$; \Cref{app:fig_gsm1k}), but the small sample requires replication.

